{"id":"7cce082e1f9c164705ac63b5","source":"guardian-rss","source_name":"The Guardian World","platform":"rss","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/sep/17/labor-immigration-policy-tony-burke-anthony-albanese-one-nation-ntwnfb","published_at":"2026-09-17T00:19:41Z","collected_at":"2026-09-17T00:27:50.887718Z","title":"Australian politics live: Lambie welcomes Labor backdown on allied health cap for veterans after emotional speech to parliament","text":"Follow the day’s news live Get our breaking news email , free app or daily news podcast Barnaby Joyce says Labor is copying and pasting One Nation’s homework because the measures we know so far around blocking the families of international students and preventing visa hopping have some similarity to what Pauline Hanson is suggesting. Speaking to the Today show a little earlier, Joyce says Burke’s last minute cancellation at the press club was “the greatest no-show since Amelia Earhart”. [Burke] can’t possibly be about to announce One Nation’s policy. I mean, Tony’s a great guy, right? I like Tony, but honestly, he was the greatest no-show since Amelia Earhart when he didn’t turn up to the Press Club … What I’ve heard thus far is that it’s an exact replication, but not to the extent of what One Nation is doing. I will be watching this with absolute fascination and some trepidation, and I’ll be waiting for Jim Chalmers to explain his new budget. I was on polling booths with my colleagues like Sally Sitou . I was out there in Reid with her in those last weeks of the campaign, standing there for hours at a time. She would be surrounded by men a foot taller than her, stopping her physic","html":"","media":[],"author":"Krishani Dhanji","language":"und","group":"international-media","perspective":"mixed","trust":"high","tags":[],"raw":{"archive_policy":"public_excerpt_v1","content_sha256":"bc48916b9c757771e2987b88b5cbb9348cc73d7567e58a28e1fc9aa9d39051af","original_text_chars":1452,"original_html_chars":1638,"text_truncated":true,"media_count":0,"platform":{"entry_id":"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/sep/17/labor-immigration-policy-tony-burke-anthony-albanese-one-nation-ntwnfb","entry_link":"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/sep/17/labor-immigration-policy-tony-burke-anthony-albanese-one-nation-ntwnfb"}}}
{"id":"8381c48198a5613884f0e6e4","source":"guardian-rss","source_name":"The Guardian World","platform":"rss","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/16/texas-georgia-executions","published_at":"2026-09-17T00:13:15Z","collected_at":"2026-09-17T00:28:03.135325Z","title":"Texas kills man by lethal injection in state’s fifth execution this year","text":"A jail cell at the state penitentiary in Huntsville, Texas. Photograph: Reuters View image in fullscreen A jail cell at the state penitentiary in Huntsville, Texas. Photograph: Reuters Capital punishment Texas kills man by lethal injection in state’s fifth execution this year LeJames Norman, 40, killed at Huntsville penitentiary while scheduled execution in Georgia halted by judge Associated Press Wed 16 Sep 2026 20.13 EDT First published on Wed 16 Sep 2026 08.32 EDT Share Prefer the Guardian on Google A man convicted over the shooting deaths of three people was executed on Wednesday in Texas, while another execution scheduled for the same time in Georgia was halted by a judge. It’s the fifth execution in Texas this year. Georgia has yet to put anyone to death this year. The nation’s busiest death chamber is in Florida, which accounted for more than half the 25 executions already carried out in the US this year. LeJames Norman, 40, was sentenced to death over the 2005 deaths of Samuel Roberts, 24, Tiffani Peacock, 18, and Celso Lopez, 38, inside the home they shared in a small, rural town. He died by injection of the sedative pentobarbital at the state penitentiary in Huntsville, T","html":"","media":["https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/1878e84a1505a49af35f5e3a3ded364a75de5c2a/179_0_1841_1473/master/1841.jpg?width=465&dpr=1&s=none&crop=none"],"author":"Associated Press","language":"und","group":"international-media","perspective":"mixed","trust":"high","tags":[],"raw":{"archive_policy":"public_excerpt_v1","content_sha256":"b0826217f26a5adf7821c0b7251d7214e40300b12752e4feae6353f4a9e18037","original_text_chars":5270,"original_html_chars":15234,"text_truncated":true,"media_count":1,"platform":{"entry_id":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/16/texas-georgia-executions","entry_link":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/16/texas-georgia-executions"}}}
